---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleHeader from '../../components/ArticleHeader.astro';
import SectionRail from '../../components/SectionRail.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../utils/url';
import '../../styles/article.css';
import StatStrip from '../../components/StatStrip.astro';
import Abstract from '../../components/Abstract.astro';
import type { ResearchMeta } from '../../types';

export const meta: ResearchMeta = {
  title: 'Building an SLM: A foot into building open source models',
  dek: 'This is an independent exercise, written as a build log, in pairing a retrieval system with a fine-tuned small language model, and exploring how open source models are becoming more accessible.',
  date: '2026-09-12',
  slug: 'building-an-slm',
  stats: [
    { value: '24', label: 'documents' },
    { value: '1,217', label: 'chunks' },
    { value: '219', label: 'training examples' },
    { value: '27/27', label: 'recall@5' },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout title={meta.title} description={meta.dek} nav="gold">
  <ArticleHeader icon={meta.slug} theme="gold" title={meta.title} backHref={withBase('/projects')} backLabel="Projects" meta={['Pivot Log · Independent research']}>
    <Fragment slot="dek">{meta.dek}</Fragment>
    <StatStrip slot="after" stats={meta.stats} />
  </ArticleHeader>

  <article class="piece">
    <SectionRail />

    <p class="intro-para">
      I built a full RAG-plus-fine-tuned-SLM pipeline for India-Switzerland
      cross-border tax questions. The basic idea was for the knowledge to
      be in a curated database chunked and retrieved and the fine-tuned
      model behaving according to my specific use case.
    </p>

    <p class="spec-line">
      24 documents &middot; 1,217 chunks &middot; 219 training examples &middot;
      Qwen3-4B fine-tuned via LoRA (rank 16) &middot; 27/27 recall@5 &middot;
      runs entirely on my own M1 Air &mdash; no GPU
    </p>

    <Abstract>
      This is my first attempt at building an open source model. I set out to
      build a RAG system for India-Switzerland cross-border tax and
      wealth-structuring questions, with a small language model fine-tuned
      layered on top to retrieve and format the output. The idea was to have
      a usable model, with verification passes, and even multiple iterations
      of training datasets to finalise the chunking system underneath.
      Currently, the corpus sits at 24 documents and 1,217 chunks, retrieval
      lands the right document in the top five on 27 of 27 labelled test
      queries. The fine-tuned model, a Qwen3-4B, trained via LoRA on a free
      Colab GPU, answers in a mandated five-part structure and also reasons
      selectively. The single most heavily weighted behaviour of the SLM, is
      that it declines honestly rather than making something up., which I
      think is crucial for an exercise like this. All of this runs on my own
      laptop, and serves as a good base to see what these models will soon be
      capable off.
    </Abstract>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="s1-heading">
      <h2 id="s1-heading">1. Why I built this</h2>
      <p>
        I built this because I was becoming increasingly aware of the
        direction we were heading with respect to open source models, and
        building and housing things locally. The acquisition of hugging
        face, Kimi K3 being entirely open source with similar capabilities
        of frontier models means that the accessibility to capable models is
        only going to compound. A handful of enterprises have already made
        this swap explicitly, a fine-tuned model a fraction of the size of a
        general frontier model, running on infrastructure they control, and
        doing one job incredibly well. Given this judgement call of where we
        are heading I wanted to start working in the direction of building,
        or even understanding these models myself.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also knew I had to understand the dynamic between how models
        behave in terms of just 'absolute' ability compared to being able to
        get targeted results with the correct architecture around it. In
        this case the correct architecture being RAG and retrieval layers,
        and a fine tuned model. It's easy to be tempted to just throw more
        data at the model, but simply put, training the model on information
        isn't really the way to get it smarter, the correct approach is to
        increase your database and then train your models behaviour
        according to this increased laye. So in essence I wanted to build
        this because I wanted an in-house reasoning tool, purpose built for
        one narrow cross-border use case, which could come in handy when
        data privacy laws become stricter or even some political
        motivations, like holding back the shipping of frontier models.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="s2-heading">
      <h2 id="s2-heading">2. Building the library</h2>
      <p>
        I started by building my corpus and the chunking mechanism for them,
        in essence how powerful and capable the model was heavily dependent
        on which documents I chose for my corpus and how they are chunked.
        The corpus building portion actually took a considerable amount of
        time, and a couple of iterations till I settled on this split:
        <em>24 documents with 1,217 chunks, DTAA treaty text, the Income-tax
        Act 2025, FEMA legislation and RBI Master Directions, Swiss
        succession, foundation and anti-money-laundering law, Swiss tax
        reference material, and case law, organised by jurisdiction</em>.
        In the end I tried covering most of the important documents that
        would be necessary for cross border transactions, and watering down
        larger documents like the general Indian tax laws to its relevant
        sections. Roughly two-thirds of the chunk volume was on the India
        side, a fifth on the Switzerland side, and the rest bilateral or
        treaty material.
      </p>
      <p>
        One interesting choice that I had to make was to see how I could get
        some of the biggest source documents into my corpus, such as a
        536-section Income-tax Act, a 422-page Rules print-export, a
        391-section Succession Act. Taken as it is, these would skew the
        chunk count in their favour, and even have a substantial amount of
        sections that wouldn't have been relevant, thus generally reducing
        accuracy. So what I did was distil them down to the cross-border-relevant
        subset before chunking them. While the corpus building was the
        slowest and arguably the hardest part of the project, I think it
        highlights the importance of piecing together a relevant and
        targeted dataset. The manual effort of data work isn't going to
        disappear, the ones that can do it the smartest will just be able to
        get the most out of their model &mdash; the context layer.
      </p>

      <h3>Moving away from Corpus v1</h3>
      <p>
        As I mentioned, the final dataset I landed on, and the specific
        chunking mechanism took a couple of rounds of iteration. I want to
        walk through some problems with my v1, as that was certainly part of
        the learning I had. The original chunker I built matched article
        headings only at the very start of a line, but when I looked at a
        few examples of the text, 'ARTICLE 13' began directly after Article
        12's last sentence. Basically it printed sentence that list multiple
        articles as its own chunk. Unfortunately my initial check and regex
        never caught it. I initially thought I'd just remove the line-start
        requirement entirely, and I thought that fixed it, ran this for like
        2 days chunking everything locally. But It didn't.
      </p>
      <p>
        So after a couple of rounds of reworking I landed on six
        structure-detecting patterns. This is the rough split of the
        chunking mechanisms for reference:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>one for numbered treaty articles,</li>
        <li>one for hand-compiled 'Art. 12'-style documents,</li>
        <li>one for FAQ-formatted RBI circulars,</li>
        <li>one for Indian legislative section numbering,</li>
        <li>one for Part/Chapter headings,</li>
        <li>one filename-scoped special case just for the Swiss Tax System's own decimal nesting.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Along with this a specific addition I made was to ensure that six
        known tax-rate tables inside the Income-tax Act get pulled out and
        protected before being chunked. This was because the internal
        numbering was a bit of a mess. I labelled every chunk with its own
        filename in its header, because section numbers overlapped but the
        distinction of where they came from was crucial.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="s3-heading">
      <h2 id="s3-heading">3. Retrieving said chunks</h2>
      <p>
        I initially built a first version which was vector-only; a pure
        semantic search over the embeddings (BAAI's small English embedding
        model). This broke quite fast because legal text was a little bit
        more complex to run this on; anything depending on an exact term or
        number rather than a general meaning fell short. So for my second
        iteration I added BM25 keyword search alongside it and fused the two
        ranked lists. This was much much better than the results I saw with
        the old vector-only retriever, so the final retrieval system was a
        vector search with BM25 layered on top of it.
      </p>
      <p>
        One addition that I also used to make my retrieval smarter was to
        have a jurisdiction-aware filtering on top of this. I built this in
        a way that this was a hard filter, but also that it was paired with
        safety net, so a bilateral questions (India and Switzerland both
        relevant at once) never gets starved by an overtly rigid filter. I
        also had a dedicated balanced_search() specifically protecting
        cross-jurisdiction queries from one side's larger document volume
        drowning out the other's smaller one, and a CrossEncoder reranking
        pass on top of everything, re-scoring the handful of results that
        make it through. The per source and per jurisdiction caps were
        numbers that I eyeballed and felt comfortable with at that time, but
        if you want a more sophisticated approach to this you can run
        different iterations as an isolated exercise on the corpus before
        layering it in.
      </p>

      <!-- Original note: "Here is a quick summary of the results of this –
           (Present this more visually, less as text)" — author's own
           production instruction, not reader-facing content, so kept out
           of the rendered page but preserved here verbatim. -->
      <p class="aside">
        What the system actually does, checked against 27 <strong>labelled</strong>
        queries &mdash; one for every document in the corpus, plus three
        targeted checks against provisions I'd specifically flagged as
        retrieval failures earlier in the project &mdash; is land the
        correct source document somewhere in the top five, every single
        time. 27 out of 27. The correct document sits in first place 26 out
        of 27 times. Two of the three previously-flagged provisions (a
        company-residency tie-breaker clause, a pensions article) now
        retrieve cleanly. The third, a royalty-rate clause, retrieves &mdash;
        but only at rank four, buried behind three chunks pulled from an
        entirely different, much larger document that happens to share the
        same treaty-adjacent vocabulary. That's not a semantic failure, it's
        a volume one: that competing document alone accounts for 121 of the
        corpus's 1,217 chunks, more than double the entire treaty document
        it's crowding out. Worth being precise about what this metric
        actually proves and doesn't: it confirms the right file made the
        shortlist, not that the specific paragraph carrying the rate itself
        was the exact chunk retrieved.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="s4-heading">
      <h2 id="s4-heading">4. Teaching it to behave</h2>
      <p>
        Now we move on to the more fun part of the build, the fine tuning of
        the model. Before I get into it I feel like it's worth being
        explicit about what fine-tuning is doing here. A small language
        model, the one I chose is about 4 billion parameters, comes out of
        its own base training knowing the basics of English and general
        reasoning. What it doesn't have, and what it's deliberately never
        given, is the actual substance or knowledge of India-Switzerland tax
        law. The fine-tune's whole job is to actually shape behaviour with
        the outputs: so answer only from what's retrieved, structure every
        answer into a fixed five-part shape, reason inside a &lt;think&gt;
        block selectively rather than constantly or never. Additionally it
        also learns (what is the single most heavily weighted thing in the
        entire training set, at just under 22% of every example), it
        declines when the retrieved context doesn't actually cover the
        question. For this exercise the training example's grounding had to
        come from a the retrieval calls, so that it understands it needs to
        reason only with my RAG output.
      </p>
      <p>
        The choice of Qwen3-4B specifically to do this was for a couple of
        reasons. It ships with native &lt;think&gt; support that matches
        this training shape out of the box, and it's small enough to
        fine-tune on a free Colab GPU and, later, run entirely on my M1.
        Additionally the Unsloth (the framework I used) has a purpose-built
        notebook for exactly this model family. So honestly, it wasn't much
        to do with capability, it was more to do with convenience and as a
        base to run my experiment on.
      </p>

      <!-- Original note: "[VISUAL — expandable panel, collapsed by
           default: a small diagram showing the full ~4-billion-parameter
           base model greyed out and frozen, with only a thin, highlighted
           sliver — the actual trainable LoRA matrices, sitting at specific
           attention and feed-forward layers — lit up and labelled 'under
           1% of the model actually trains.' Meant as a quick, optional
           stop for anyone who wants the visual before reading on, not a
           mandatory read. Placeholder — final visual to be decided.]"
           Author's own production note — excluded from the rendered page,
           kept here verbatim. -->

      <p class="aside">
        Practically: LoRA freezes the entire base model and instead trains
        small, low-rank adapter matrices bolted onto specific attention and
        feed-forward layers, under 1% of the model's total parameters
        actually move. A setting called train_on_responses_only masks the
        loss so the model is only ever penalised for getting its own words
        wrong, never for the system prompt or the retrieved context it was
        handed, which would otherwise waste training signal teaching the
        model to predict text it was never meant to generate in the first
        place. Rank, how large those adapter matrices are, stayed at 16
        across both training runs I ran; when the second run's
        reasoning-block discipline actually got worse rather than better, I
        deliberately didn't raise rank as a fix, because the problem looked
        like a training-exposure issue, not a capacity one, and turning
        that dial up risked quietly breaking the formatting compliance that
        was already sitting at a clean 35 out of 35.
      </p>

      <p>
        As I touched upon in my chunking section, the corpus and thus also
        the training dataset went through a couple of iterations. This was
        the most painful decision because I threw away 200 training examples
        which took me a considerable time to piece together. I then rebuilt
        and landed at 219 examples. I used Weights and Biases to track my
        training, looking at loss curves, eval runs etc.
      </p>

      <!-- Original note: "Explain how loss curves and eval runs work –
           attach screenshots." — author's own production note, excluded
           from the rendered page, kept here verbatim. -->

      <p>
        While the graphs did look nice, the loss curves didn't help my
        understanding of specific nuances such as if my &lt;think&gt; block
        was behaving the way I wanted it to. By design, both runs produced
        perfectly normal loss curves, so in the end the &lt;think&gt; block
        check needed a full, manual read-through of all 35 holdout examples.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="s5-heading">
      <h2 id="s5-heading">5. Getting it running</h2>
      <p>
        After fine tuning my model, and setting up the dataset, RAG and
        everything. I had to figure out how to house and be able to call
        and run this model. This part is just the technical specs, so if it
        interests you keep reading, or skim over this next paragraph. The
        path I took was: LoRA adapter, reloaded and merged into the base
        model at full 16-bit precision (not the 4-bit precision training
        itself used), then quantized down to a much smaller GGUF file at
        q4_k_m precision. This was specifically so my 4-billion-parameter
        model could run at a reasonable speed on my laptop, then I loaded it
        into Ollama, and finally wired into the actual retrieval pipeline.
        Also just pointing out that the 4-bit one during training is purely
        a memory trick to fit inside Colab's free GPU tier, and the q4_k_m
        one after training is purely a deployment-size trick. Lots of small
        workarounds to utilise the infrastructure I have most efficiently.
      </p>
      <p>
        The finished thing runs entirely on my laptop. When I load this up
        for a fresh run, it takes about 7 seconds of retrieval and 47
        seconds of generation, roughly 54 seconds end to end. I will say
        it's not the fastest, but pretty reasonable given the specs. I did
        also run a test by pushing it across multiple queries and what I saw
        was it doesn't hold as well. Across ten queries run back to back
        with no recovery time between them, retrieval slowed by 5.6 times
        and generation by 2.2 times by the eighth to tenth query. Finally, I
        designed the dashboard on Streamlit, very basic UX, choosing a
        colour scheme that looked semi-professional for this exercise and
        an output format that you'd be able to read.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="s6-heading">
      <h2 id="s6-heading">6. How useful is this model</h2>
      <p>
        Well to be very honest, with this first attempt I wouldn't use it to
        make any decisions for me. A frontier model is just so much more
        superior (and moving that much faster), and any open source local
        model trained like this isn't going to be able to compete, you'd
        need much more powerful GPUs and holistic datasets etc. But that
        being said, it was pretty reliable in terms of consistency of
        output. It is also a really good tool to have If client information
        has various data privacy laws that require it to be housed locally.
      </p>
      <p>
        In essence this is just an in-house reasoning tool for one specific
        cross-border tax relationship, not at all something trying to
        compete with a frontier model's raw factual coverage. What this
        tool does very well that it answers in a structured and repeatable
        fashion. In terms of results, the right source document lands in
        its top five results essentially every time, and first, correctly,
        in all but one case that I tested. And I daresay most importantly it
        is heavily trained to have the ability to say it doesn't know rather
        than confidently make something up, which is the entire point of
        teaching a small model behaviour.
      </p>
      <p>
        Because this was an exercise on trying to build out an SLM, I want
        to emphasise the stuff it didn't do as well, so when I do run a
        second run of a similar build, or consulting at an enterprise
        level, these are something I am going to be looking out for.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          One treaty provision, a royalty rate clause, retrieves correctly
          but ranks lower than it should, specifically because a much
          larger, tangentially related document in the same jurisdiction
          crowds the same top-five slots on similar vocabulary. I explored
          and think the fix is a corpus-balancing problem, not really a
          retrieval-logic one.
        </li>
        <li>
          The model can correctly flag when a transaction size exceeds a
          regulatory cap and recommend restructuring, but only when a
          question explicitly asks whether the standard route is
          sufficient. It needs simple 'how do I move this much money'
          question, and doesn't spontaneously trigger that comparison on
          its own, it stops at a qualitative warning rather than computing
          the implied timescale. This added quantitative judgment is
          something I considered training separately and then adding on
        </li>
        <li>
          And its selective use of the reasoning block, while reliable on
          straightforward formatting, is a bit more inconsistent on the
          harder judgment of when a question actually needs multi-step
          reasoning versus a direct lookup.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>Good to note, good to keep in mind.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="s7-heading">
      <h2 id="s7-heading">7. Great, now where does this go</h2>
      <p>
        I think the reason this kind of project is going to matter more,
        over the next year is fairly simple. Throwing a frontier model's API
        at every problem is convenient, but it's not always the right tool,
        and for a narrow, well-defined, repeatable job, a small model that
        can run entirely on your own hardware is often the better choice. In
        this case data never leaves your infrastructure, the models
        behaviour is yours to shape and constrain exactly the way you need
        it to, and you won't pay tokens for a capability that you won't
        need. What I want to reiterate is that I don't think that means big
        general models stop mattering, they're always going to be one of
        the most powerful reasoning tools, but I do think the instinct to
        reach for one by default, for every problem, regardless of scope, is
        going to look increasingly lazy. And companies are going to slowly
        start drifting away from that.
      </p>
      <p>
        On a personal level this was a project to understand the difference
        between a model knowing something and a model behaving correctly
        around something it's been handed. I managed to train, run and host
        an SLM to cater to my specific use case, and I will be trying this
        same exercises on more targeted requirements and stronger machines.
        I do find that this project has equipped me enough to, down the
        line, start training and housing my own models. And when a
        disgustingly (conscious word choice) strong open source model from
        China, maybe Kimi 75 rolls around, I am going to fine tune that to
        just run whatever I need to. This is the democratisation of
        capability that I have been screaming about from the rooftops.
        You'll see. For now we have a working system, that knows what it
        doesn't know and does what it's been taught.
      </p>
      <p class="credits">
        Built solo. Qwen3-4B, fine-tuned via LoRA (rank 16) on Google
        Colab's free T4 tier &middot; hybrid BM25 + dense retrieval with
        jurisdiction-aware filtering and a CrossEncoder reranker, over 24
        documents and 1,217 chunks in ChromaDB &middot; deployed locally via
        Ollama, CPU only.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
